  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "High-Pass Behavior",
    "lesson_core_move": "Classify high-pass behavior for an LTI filter from its magnitude response by comparing |$H(j\\omega)$| at low and high frequencies and identifying low-frequency attenuation with high-frequency passage.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Behavior",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to High-Pass Behavior",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "High-pass behavior means an LTI filter attenuates low-frequency components and preserves high-frequency components. In a magnitude response, that shows up as a small value of $|H(j\\omega)|$ near $\\omega=0$ and a larger value at higher $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The test is a comparison between the low-frequency end and the high-frequency end of $|H(j\\omega)|$. If the curve is lower near $\\omega=0$ than it is farther to the right, the response is moving in the high-pass direction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_{\\text{low}})| \\ll |H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j0)| \\approx 0, \\qquad |H(j\\omega)| \\text{ grows with } \\omega",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The clean prototype is a filter whose gain is near $0$ at low frequency and near $1$ at high frequency. That is the simplest model of high-pass behavior before the curve gets more complicated.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the decision rule is simple: if the low-frequency gain is smaller than the high-frequency gain, classify the magnitude response as high-pass. Later sections will apply the same comparison to specific curves.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Behavior",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Low-Frequency Attenuation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A filter has $|H(j\\omega)| \\approx 0.15$ for small $\\omega$. What does that say about low-frequency components?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the small value of $|H(j\\omega)|$ near $\\omega=0$. A gain of about $0.15$ is well below $1$, so the low-frequency end of the response is attenuating the signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j0)| \\approx 0.15 < 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That means a low-frequency component comes out with a much smaller amplitude than it went in. In other words, the system suppresses low-frequency content rather than passing it through unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the low-frequency side of a high-pass response: the low end is small, so the filter is already showing attenuation where $\\omega$ is near zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Behavior",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Recognizing High-Frequency Passage",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A filter has $|H(j\\omega)| \\approx 0.95$ for large $\\omega$. What does that say about high-frequency components?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the large-$\\omega$ end of the magnitude response. A value of $|H(j\\omega)| \\approx 0.95$ is close to $1$, so the high-frequency side is not being cut down very much.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})| \\approx 0.95 \\approx 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That means a high-frequency component passes through with almost the same amplitude it had at the input. The response is showing high-frequency passage rather than strong suppression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Do not call this amplification just because the component is passed. Passage means the amplitude is essentially preserved; amplification would require a gain above $1$. This is the high-frequency side of a high-pass response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Behavior",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Comparing Low- and High-Frequency Gain",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A filter has $|H(j\\omega)| \\approx 0.2$ near $\\omega=0$ and $|H(j\\omega)| \\approx 1.1$ for large $\\omega$. Compare the gains and state what the comparison means.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the pair of gains: $0.2$ near $\\omega=0$ and $1.1$ for large $\\omega$. Read them as the low-frequency gain and the high-frequency gain, then compare them directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_{\\text{low}})| \\approx 0.2,\\qquad |H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})| \\approx 1.1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The high-frequency gain is larger because $1.1 > 0.2$. The low-frequency side is well below $1$, so low frequencies are attenuated, while the high-frequency side is near $1$ and passes more strongly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That means the magnitude response rises from low to high frequency, so the filter behaves like a high-pass filter.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Behavior",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Classifying a Magnitude Response as High-Pass",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A filter has $|H(j\\omega)|$ small near $\\omega=0$ and near $1$ for large $\\omega$. Classify the filter from its magnitude response.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the pair of ends of the magnitude response: the low-frequency side is small and the high-frequency side is near $1$. Compare those two ends directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_{\\text{low}})| \\ll 1,\\qquad |H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})| \\approx 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the low-frequency gain is much smaller than the high-frequency gain, low frequencies are attenuated while high frequencies pass. The response is not flat, so it is not all-pass.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That overall pattern is high-pass: the magnitude response suppresses low frequencies and preserves high frequencies, so the correct classification is high-pass rather than low-pass or band-pass.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
